-- Template for configs/gpu.lua, which is per-machine and gitignored.
--
-- install/gpu.sh copies this file to gpu.lua and appends the profile that fits
-- the machine; on a machine without an NVIDIA GPU nothing is needed and the
-- copy stays inert. To choose by hand, copy this file to gpu.lua and
-- UNCOMMENT EXACTLY ONE profile.
--
-- Which profile applies comes down to one question -- which GPU are the displays
-- wired to? Check with:
--   for c in /sys/class/drm/card*-*/status; do printf '%-24s %s\n' "${c%/status}" "$(cat $c)"; done
--   cat /sys/class/drm/card1/device/vendor   # 0x10de NVIDIA, 0x1002 AMD, 0x8086 Intel
-- The card that owns the *connected* connectors is the one that scans out.

-- --- Profile A: displays wired to an AMD/Intel iGPU (muxless laptop) ---------
-- Clients must render on the iGPU that scans out. Sending them to the dGPU adds
-- a cross-GPU dmabuf import per frame, which can leave windows rendering as
-- garbled shrunken text after the dGPU cycles its VRAM. dGPU via prime-run.
-- hl.env("LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME", "radeonsi")        -- Intel iGPU: iHD
-- hl.env("WLR_NO_HARDWARE_CURSORS", "1")

-- --- Profile B: displays wired to the NVIDIA GPU (typical desktop) ----------
-- Hyprland itself runs on NVIDIA, so there is no cross-GPU path at all and
-- prime-run is pointless. VA-API goes through libva-nvidia-driver (RPM
-- Fusion, installed by install/gpu.sh); without it LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=nvidia
-- makes libva fail outright (va_openDriver returns -1) instead of falling back.
-- MOZ_DISABLE_RDD_SANDBOX lets Firefox reach the driver for video decoding.
-- hl.env("LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME", "nvidia")
-- hl.env("NVD_BACKEND", "direct")
-- hl.env("MOZ_DISABLE_RDD_SANDBOX", "1")
